To create a new directory on GitHub, you can use one of these methods:


Method 1: Create a Directory via New File (Easiest)
Navigate to your repository on GitHub.
Click the Add file button and select Create new file.
In the filename input box, type the name of your new directory followed by a forward slash (e.g., MyNewFolder/).
GitHub will automatically recognize this as a folder and create it.
Since Git directories cannot be empty, you must also name a file inside it (e.g., MyNewFolder/.gitkeep or MyNewFolder/README.md).
Scroll down and click Commit new file.

Method 2: Upload a Folder
Navigate to your repository on GitHub.
Click Add file > Upload files.
Drag and drop a folder from your computer into the upload area.
Click Commit changes.

Method 3: Using the Web Editor (for existing repos)
If you are already editing a file or viewing the repo:
Click the . key on your keyboard to open the web-based code editor.
Right-click in the file explorer sidebar and select New Folder.
Name the folder and ensure you add at least one file inside it before committing.
Note: Git does not track empty directories. You must always have at least one file (commonly a .gitkeep file) inside the directory for it to be saved in the repository.

